# G006 — Mortgage Calculator

*v2. See `mortgage.md` for prose: every temporal `where` is amortized —
not "did we hit the target" but "are we tracking toward it." The
pace check turns a monthly obligation into a daily gradient.*

---

## Choreographic Case: Kathryn's Daily Finance Where

[daily-finance-where @today
    IN ORDER TO
        evaluate whether today's cumulative P&L keeps the month on track
        so a distant monthly obligation becomes a daily gradient
        and adjustment is continuous, not an end-of-month shock

    @pace <- [pace-check
                {target: @em:monthly-costs,
                 periods: @month:trading-days,
                 current: @today:day-number,
                 cumulative: @month:cumulative-pnl}]

    @kathryn{evaluate @pace against acceptable variance}
        where on-track     -> -> #on-track
        where off-pace     -> -> #adjust-strategy
]

---

## Verb Surface

[monthly-payment
    {principal, rate, years}                ;; -> payment
]

[amortization
    {principal, rate, years}                 ;; -> [Payment]
]

[pace-check
    {target, periods, current, cumulative}   ;; -> Pace
]

---

## Distinctive Innate idiom: amortised `where` as periodic gradient

[pace-check {target: @t, periods: @n, current: @i, cumulative: @c}]
    IN ORDER TO
        turn an end-of-period obligation into a per-period score
        so "are we on track" has an answer every day, not just month-end

    @expected <- @t × (@i / @n)
    @pace-ratio <- @c / @expected
        where @pace-ratio at least 1.0  -> on-track
        where @pace-ratio at least 0.8  -> acceptable-variance
        otherwise                        -> off-pace
]

Same primitive at every level of the temporal chain: daily against
weekly, weekly against monthly, monthly against quarterly. The
mortgage math underneath every temporal `where` in the noosphere.

---

## Demo

[demo
    -> [monthly-payment {principal: 300000, rate: 0.065, years: 30}]
    ;; -> 1896.20

    -> [pace-check
          {target: 10000, periods: 20, current: 5, cumulative: 2300}]
    ;; -> {expected: 2500, ratio: 0.92, status: "acceptable-variance"}

    -> [pace-check
          {target: 10000, periods: 20, current: 5, cumulative: 500}]
    ;; -> {expected: 2500, ratio: 0.20, status: "off-pace"}
]
